          a: `This project looks at mental health at two levels:\n\n1. **Workplace**: the OSMI *Mental Health in Tech* survey (1,258 respondents, 2014–2016): who has sought treatment, and how workplace support relates to it.\n2. **National**: World Bank suicide mortality for 185 countries, 2000–2021, with income, alcohol, health-spending, education and employment indicators, plus models that test how predictable national rates are.\n\n**Key findings:** suicide rates fell 27% across 185 countries from 2000 to 2021 (World Bank / WHO, indicator SH.STA.SUIC.P5) but rose in North and Latin America; alcohol is the strongest country-level correlate after controlling for region; richer countries record higher rates, yet within a country growing richer goes with falling rates. In the survey, family history is the strongest predictor of seeking treatment (adjusted odds ratio 4.5), men have less than half the odds of women, and workplace support still matters after adjustment.\n\nIt's population-level analysis, not a tool for assessing any individual's risk.`,
